<p>
    <strong>Core Gameplan:</strong> Orzhov control leans on hand disruption
    (<a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Gerrard's Verdict">Gerrard's Verdict</a
    >, <a role="button" tabindex="0" class="card-hover" data-card-name="Duress"
        >Duress</a
    >) backed by flexible permanent removal (<a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Vindicate">Vindicate</a
    >, <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Swords to Plowshares"
        >Swords to Plowshares</a
    >). The thing is, their discard actually helps us: every enchantment they
    make us pitch just gets our graveyard closer to a game-winning <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Replenish">Replenish</a
    >.
</p>
<p>
    <strong>Sideboard Strategy:</strong> Expect graveyard hate (<a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Withered Wretch">Withered Wretch</a
    >, <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Tormod's Crypt">Tormod's Crypt</a
    >) and enchantment taxes (<a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Aura of Silence">Aura of Silence</a
    >). Bring in <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Exalted Angel">Exalted Angel</a
    > as a morph threat they'll struggle to answer, plus instant-speed
    removal like <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Disenchant">Disenchant</a
    > and <a
        role="button" tabindex="0"
        class="card-hover"
        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
    >. Don't sit in a long standoff—push the game while their removal is
    pointed at the wrong things.
</p>
